## How do local SEO services compare to national SEO in Great Britain?

Local SEO helps one business show up in one area. It works mainly through Google Business Profile and the map pack. National SEO helps a business rank for searches anywhere in the UK. It works mainly through the website itself. A multi-location business needs both at once.

| | Local SEO | National SEO | Multi-location SEO |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Typical business | One branch or office | Sells UK-wide, often online | Many branches, franchises or service areas |
| Searches targeted | "plumber in Reading", "near me" | "commercial cleaning contracts", "buy oak flooring" | Both, per branch and per service |
| Main levers | Google Business Profile, reviews, local pages | Site architecture, topical depth, links, technical health | Location pages, profiles for every branch, central architecture |
| Main risk | Weak or unverified profile | Thin category pages, competing with large brands | Doorway pages and inconsistent branch details |
| My service | [Local SEO](/services/local-seo/), from £600 per month | National retainer, from £1,500 per month | National retainer, scoped by number of locations |

Local SEO is the cheaper and better fit for one location that sells locally. National SEO makes sense when your customers are spread across the country or you run more than a handful of branches.

## Who does national SEO suit?

National SEO suits businesses whose revenue does not depend on one town:

- **Multi-location service businesses:** trades, clinics, lettings, legal or accountancy firms with several offices
- **Franchises:** a central brand with franchisee branches that each need local visibility
- **Ecommerce:** online shops that sell and deliver across the UK
- **B2B firms:** suppliers, manufacturers and consultancies that win contracts nationally

An [international SEO](/services/international-seo-consultant/) scope fits better when the challenge is many countries rather than many UK locations.

## How do you build location pages that are not doorway pages?

Location pages avoid the doorway label when each one carries facts only that branch can give. Doorway pages are near-identical pages made to rank for different town names. Each one funnels people to the same place. A page that only swaps the town name in a template is the classic example.

Google lists doorway abuse as a violation in its [spam policies](https://developers.google.com/search/docs/essentials/spam-policies#doorways). The two panels below show the difference I check before a location page goes live.

{% panels %}
{% panel eyebrow="Location page" title="A page that earns its place" tone="good" %}
- **A real place:** a branch address, a staffed service area or a named team that covers it
- **Local facts:** opening hours, the people who work there, parking, coverage limits and local prices where they differ
- **Local proof:** reviews from that branch's Google Business Profile and photos taken there
- **A clear job:** the services that branch offers, each linking to the national service page
- **Consistent details:** the same name, address and phone number as the profile and the main directories
{% /panel %}
{% panel eyebrow="Doorway page" title="A page Google's spam policy targets" tone="bad" %}
- **A swapped town name:** the same template copy with only the place changed
- **No presence:** no branch, staff or service area in that town
- **Nothing local:** no hours, team, reviews or prices specific to the place
- **One funnel:** every page sends people to the same contact form
- **Volume over use:** dozens of pages created for rankings rather than for a visitor
{% /panel %}
{% /panels %}

I do not create a page for a town with no real branch or service presence. A service-area section on a nearby branch page is safer and more honest.

## How do you manage Google Business Profiles for a franchise or many branches?

Google Business Profiles for many branches are managed through one owner account, shared naming rules and a monthly check. Each genuine branch can have its own profile. Managing them at scale is about control and consistency more than tricks:

- one owner account with clear manager access for each branch
- the same naming rules, primary category and service list across branches (adjusted where a branch really differs)
- each profile linking to its own location page rather than the homepage
- a review process every branch follows and replies from someone who can act on them
- a monthly check for duplicate profiles, wrong pins and suggested edits from the public

Every branch profile needs verification. A franchise needs a clear plan for who verifies and manages each one. I include that plan in the first month of a national SEO retainer.

## What site architecture do national rankings need?

National rankings come mostly from the website. A business competing with large brands needs a clear structure for every service or product category and enough depth on each one to show expertise.

I map the categories and services into a hierarchy. I then plan supporting guides for the questions buyers ask before they choose. This builds topical authority. Each page has one job. Related pages link to each other. No two pages compete for the same search. A [topical map](/services/topical-maps/) is the planning document for that structure as a standalone project. It costs from £800.

## How do you measure national SEO visibility?

I measure national SEO visibility in three ways:

1. **Google Search Console by page group:** clicks and impressions split into service pages, category pages, location pages and guides. You see which part of the site is growing.
2. **A fixed keyword set:** the same agreed list of national and branch searches tracked every month. Changes stay comparable month to month.
3. **Enquiries and revenue by page group:** form fills, calls and sales tied back to the pages that produced them. Missing enquiry tracking is the first job to fix.

I report the numbers with their source and date range. I do not guarantee rankings. They also depend on competitors and on Google.

